<title>BELGIUM: EU rejects modified cotton dumping plan.</title>
<headline>EU rejects modified cotton dumping plan.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-05-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>European Union member states rejected on Friday a modified plan for definitive anti-dumping duties on cotton imports from China, Egypt, India, Indonesia, Turkey and Pakistan, an EU diplomat said.</p>
<p>But talks continued in an attempt to broker a compromise proposal on duties, at France's insistence, despite a rejection of the plan by eight of the EU's 15 member countries, the diplomat added.  </p>
<p>National capitals had until 1600 (1400 GMT) on Friday to accept or reject the European Commission's modified proposal for duties on unbleached cotton fabric imports from the countries named.</p>
<p>The EU five countries supporting the plan were Belgium, Greece, Spain, Italy and Portugal, the diplomat said, while France and Luxembourg gave no reply.</p>
<p>The EU ambassadors seemed set to reject the plan after a first meeting on Thursday but after a second meeting later in the day a Commission spokesman said there was &quot;quite a strong likelihood&quot; of agreement.</p>
<p>The EU has until May 20 to confirm provisional duties imposed by the European Commission last November, setting in train the further review period after which duties lapse if not rendered definitive.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom 32 2 287 6800</p>
